<!-- Rendered by tools/build_packages.py - do not edit the generated copy. -->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>{{CHOCO_ID}}</id>
    <version>{{VERSION}}</version>
    <packageSourceUrl>{{REPO_URL}}/tree/master/packaging/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <owners>{{PUBLISHER}}</owners>
    <title>{{APP_NAME}}</title>
    <authors>{{PUBLISHER}}</authors>
    <projectUrl>{{PROJECT_URL}}</projectUrl>
    <iconUrl>{{ICON_URL}}</iconUrl>
    <copyright>{{COPYRIGHT}}</copyright>
    <licenseUrl>{{LICENSE_URL}}</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <projectSourceUrl>{{REPO_URL}}</projectSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>{{REPO_URL}}#readme</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>{{REPO_URL}}/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <tags>network testing qa latency packet-loss bandwidth windivert admin</tags>
    <summary>{{TAGLINE}}</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
{{DESCRIPTION}}

Bean Network Tester adds latency, jitter, packet loss, corruption, duplication and
bandwidth limits to the traffic of a chosen application, so you can see how it behaves
on a bad connection. It has a window and a command line, and the command line reports
every outcome as an exit code, which is what makes it usable from a pipeline.

Notes for this package:

* It needs Administrator rights to capture traffic, and it asks for them itself.
* It carries the WinDivert driver, which is what lets it intervene in real traffic.
* Your profiles, window state and CSV exports are kept in %LOCALAPPDATA%\{{TOOL_ID}},
  not in the package folder, so upgrading this package cannot take them with it.
* Impairment aimed at nothing in particular affects every connection on the machine,
  including the one you are reading this over. Aim it at an application or a port.

License: {{LICENSE}}.
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>{{RELEASE_NOTES_URL}}</releaseNotes>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
